        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">systemic governance</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">transformation governance</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Transformative governance is a proactive, systemic approach to addressing profound societal and ecological transitions, such as pandemics, climate change, and economic shifts, by fundamentally reshaping existing systems towards a sustainable common good. It crucially involves the inclusive engagement of diverse civil society actors, alongside academia, industry, and government (the &quot;quadruple helix&quot;), in co-creating solutions and driving a just transition that balances social, environmental, and digital dimensions.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Transformative governance is a new approach to respond to continuous and profound changes humanity is facing, such as pandemics, climate change, and financial crises. The approach emphasises a &quot;just triple transition&quot; that balances social, environmental (green), and digital dimensions, demanding a radical shift from traditional boundaries to a holistic perspective involving  involves academia, industry, government, and civil society.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1764 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1764">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1739"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">just transition fund</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">JTF</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Just Transition Fund</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The Just Transition Fund (JTF) is a new financial tool under the EU&apos;s cohesion policy, designed to help regions grappling with significant social and economic difficulties as they shift to a climate-neutral economy. Its core purpose is to ensure that the ambitious goals of the European Green Deal (i.e., making the EU climate-neutral by 2050) are met in a fair way, without leaving any communities behind. The JTF offers grants to regions and areas particularly vulnerable to this transition, especially those heavily reliant on fossil fuels like coal, peat, and oil shale, or those with industries that produce a lot of greenhouse gases.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">The Just Transition Fund (JTF) is a key financial instrument of the European Union. Its primary goal is to ensure that the transition towards a climate-neutral economy by 2050 is fair and leaves no one behind, particularly supporting regions, industries, and workers most affected by the shift away from fossil fuels and carbon-intensive activities.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1765 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1765">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1562"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q886"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">unemployment support</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">income support to the unemployed</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">statutory unemployment insurance</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">unemployment benefit schemes</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">unemployment benefits</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">unemployment insurance</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">unemployment payments</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Unemployment benefit schemes provide income support, usually over a limited period, to those who face temporary unemployment. The objective is to provide at least partial income replacement, enabling the beneficiary to maintain a certain standard of living during the transition period until a new employment is available.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Money that the government pays to unemployed people who are looking for a job</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1766 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1766">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1562"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1809"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">conditional cash transfer programmes</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">conditional cash transfer</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">conditional cash transfer programs</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">conditional cash transfer schemes</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">conditional cash transfers</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Conditional Cash Transfer (CCT) programs are a type of social safety net intervention designed to alleviate poverty and promote human capital development by providing regular financial payments directly to low-income households, contingent upon their fulfillment of specific conditions. These conditions typically relate to investments in human capital, such as ensuring children&apos;s regular school attendance, adhering to preventative health check-ups and vaccinations, and participating in nutrition education or other social development activities. The aim is to provide immediate poverty relief while simultaneously breaking the intergenerational cycle of poverty by fostering long-term improvements in health, education, and overall well-being.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Conditional cash transfer (CCT) programs aim to reduce poverty by making cash transfers conditional upon the receivers&apos; actions. The government only transfers the money to persons who meet certain criteria</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1767 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1767">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q53"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">access to healthcare</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">access to health care</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">aged care</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">fair health outcomes</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">just health care</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">just health outcomes</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">just healthcare</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">universal health care system</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">universal health coverage</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">universal healthcare system</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1649"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Healthcare in the just transition must also ensure equitable and continuous access to quality healthcare for communities and workers most affected by the economic shifts away from carbon-intensive industries, including robust mental health support, thereby guaranteeing that health outcomes and access to care remain just and do not exacerbate existing or create new inequalities during this profound societal and economic transformation</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Healthcare system ensures equitable access to quality care for communities and workers impacted by economic shifts, prioritising their well-being and needs. This means addressing potential negative impacts of climate action and economic restructuring on healthcare access and quality for those most vulnerable</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q572"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">solid biofuel</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solid bio-mass</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solid biofuels</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solid biomass</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Solid biofuels include crops and animal products in the form of solids. Solid biofuels also include secondary energy carriers, like charcoal and biomass pellets and briquettes. The IRENA classification of biofuels groups them as primary or secondary products, with primary products also divided by source. Splitting primary solid biofuels by source reflects interest in the environmental impacts of the different ways biological resources enter the bioenergy production chain. In energy statistics, solid biofuels should be reported in tonnes, and the energy content should be reported in joules based on measured net calorific values. Biomass pellets and briquettes, as well as charcoal, can be produced with any primary solid biofuel. The sum of all primary and secondary solid biofuels yields the total solid biofuels. When considering secondary products in the energy balance, transformation processes must be accounted for to avoid double counting or the mixing of higher energy content products with lower-energy unprocessed products. Where detailed statistics about the use of recovered wood products for energy are available, these amounts can be counted in this category if they are not included in renewable waste.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Any solid non-fossil material of biological origin used solely for energy purposes.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1769 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1769">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q572"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">liquid biofuel</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">liquid biofuels</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Liquid biofuel is mainly used as transport fuel but can also be used for electricity generation (e.g. in diesel generators) or for heating. Liquid biofuels include various alcohols, fatty acid methyl or ethyl esters, synthetic hydrocarbons and vegetable oils used directly as fuel. They are often blended with liquid fossil fuels such as petroleum gasoline and diesel. In these cases, only the biofuel component should be counted as renewable energy. This category comprises biogasoline, biodiesel, bio-jet kerosene and other liquid biofuels. The division into these groups is based on the biofuel’s suitability to blend with similar fossil fuels. Liquid biofuel is primary if the biomass feedstock used to produce it is not part of the energy system and is thus out of the scope of energy statistics (e.g. non-energy crops, waste products or new energy pathways). It is secondary if the biomass feedstock used to produce it is an energy product or biomass that would otherwise be used for energy purposes (e.g. energy crops or wood fuel). When secondary, energy statistics should include the biomass feedstock as a primary energy product, and then record the transformation of the feedstock into the specific liquid or gas biofuel in question.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Liquid derived from biomass that are used as fuels.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q177 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q177">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1883"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">fog harvesting</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">fog harvesting</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">harvest fog</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">harvesting of fog</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P4 rdf:resource="https://tech-action.unepccc.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2019/05/tna-guidebook-adaptationagriculture.pdf"/>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Fogs have the potential to provide an alternative source of fresh water in dry regions and can be harvested through the use of simple and low-cost collection systems. Captured water can then be used for agricultural irrigation and domestic use. Fog harvesting provides a way of capturing vital water supplies to support farming.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1770 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1770">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q572"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">gas biofuel</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">bio-based gas</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biogas</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">gas biofuels</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">renewable gas</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Gas biofuels are primarily used for heat and power production, but they can also serve as a transport fuel and, in small quantities, as a chemical feedstock for non-energy use. When gas biofuels are mixed with natural gas, only the biological component should be considered as renewable energy. Most gas biofuels are treated as primary energy products, and the energy content of the solid materials used to produce gas biofuels is not recorded in energy balances. Solid biofuels and renewable waste used to manufacture biogas from thermal processes should be shown as a transformation flow, accounting for transformation losses. Division of gas biofuel is based on a mix between the process used to produce them (e.g. anaerobic digestion or thermal processes) and their chemical characteristics (e.g. biomethane, biohydrogen). Gas biolfuels are mostly primary energy products. Gas biofuel is primary if the biomass feedstock used to produce it is not part of the energy system and is thus out of the scope of energy statistics (e.g. non-energy crops, waste products or new energy pathways). It is secondary if the biomass feedstock used to produce it is an energy product or biomass that would otherwise be used for energy purposes (e.g. energy crops or wood fuel). When secondary, energy statistics should include the biomass feedstock as a primary energy product, and then record the transformation of the feedstock into the specific liquid or gas biofuel in question.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Gas produced from the anaerobic digestion or thermal processes of some solid or liquid biofuels and biological residues.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1771 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1771">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q606"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">mixed hydropower</rdfs:label>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Water can be pumped into reservoirs used only for the purpose of mixed hydropower (pure pumped hydro), or it can be combined with reservoir hydropower by pumping water into reservoirs that also capture naturally flowing water. Electricity from flowing water that has previously been pumped into reservoirs is not counted as renewable energy but is subtracted from the amount of energy used for pumping, and the net energy consumption is counted as own-use of electricity for the energy industry. During periods of low demand or excess generation from other renewable sources, the plant can use excess electricity to pump water uphill to the reservoir, effectively storing energy for later use. When demand increases or other renewable sources are not available, the stored water can be released to turn turbines and generate electricity. In electricity generation statistics, production from mixed plants is divided into the proportion coming from naturally flowing water and the proportion coming from pumped hydro. The latter is counted as electricity generation from pumped hydro.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Electrical energy generated by combining pure hydropower with pumped hydro or other technologies.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1772 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1772">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q615"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">wave energy</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">wave electricity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">wave power</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">wave renewable electricity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">wave renewable energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">wave renewable power</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Most wave energy conversion technologies use floating devices that may or may not be anchored to the seabed. These devices move up and down with the waves, using this movement to push water or compressed air through turbines or convert the fluids’ momentum into rotational motion through rack and pinion mechanisms. Other wave energy converters include devices that capture the water at the tops of waves to create short-term differences in water levels (and use this to drive water turbines) and devices on the seabed that use differences in water pressure to drive turbines as waves pass over them. Specific technologies used to harvest wave energy include (a) point absorbers, which are floating buoys; (b) oscillating wave surge converters, which use an oscillating flap to capture energy while mounted on the seabed; and (c) oscillating water columns, which are partially submerged, hollow columns that compress air pockets through an air turbine.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Electricity produced from devices driven by the motion of waves.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1773 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1773">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q615"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">tidal energy</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">kinetic electricity from tides</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">kinetic energy from tides</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">kinetic power from tides</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">tidal current electricity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">tidal current energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">tidal current power</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">tidal electricity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">tidal power</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">tidal renewable electricity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">tidal renewable energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">tidal renewable power</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">tidal stream energy</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Tidal energy can be subdivided into (a) rise and fall tides and (b) ebb and flood currents. Tides involve ocean water moving vertically due to the gravitational pull of the sun and of the moon, and those pulls’ interactions with Earth’s gravitational field. Tides occur twice a day and depend on the Earth’s location and position. Tidal energy is predictable and is a constant source of energy suitable for producing baseload power. Tidal energy is usually generated close (&lt;20km) to the coastline.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Electricity produced by harnessing kinetic energy from tides.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1774 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1774">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q615"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">ocean current energy</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">kinetic marine current energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">kinetic marine current power</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">marine current energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">marine current power</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">ocean current power</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">ocean flow energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">ocean flow power</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">underwater current energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">underwater current power</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Energy is usually harvested from ocean currents in shallow water and driven by wind flows, density changes, salinity and temperature, with the water flowing in one direction either permanently or over long periods of time. Ocean current energy is typically generated more than 20 km offshore at typical depths over 500m. It&apos;s a constant source of energy suitable for baseload electricity generation. In theory, ebb and flood currents are within the scope of ocean currents, but IRENA categorises them as tidal energy due to their proximity to land.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Electricity produced by harnessing continuous flows of ocean waters.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1775 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1775">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q615"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">salinity gradient energy</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">osmotic energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">osmotic power</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">salinity energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">salinity gradient power</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">salinity power</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">salt gradient energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">salt gradient power</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Energy produced this way depends on a phenomenon that occurs when two different solutions mix. In this case, the two solutions are fresh water and salt water, for which the main difference is their salinity concentration. The two main mechanisms for generating salinity gradient energy are pressure-retarded osmosis and reversed electrodialysis. In pressure-retarded osmosis, water with a low salt content moves through membranes through osmotic permeation to reduce the salinity of salt water on the other side of the membranes and then expands inside a turbine to generate electricity. In reversed electrodialysis, the ions in salt water are used to generate an electric charge, which can be used as electricity. Other mechanisms for capturing and using these forces, such as capacity mixing, are also being researched. Salinity gradient is especially useful where rivers connect to the open ocean. Salinity gradient electricity generation has transformation losses, so total energy supply and electricity production will differ. However, the efficiency of such systems is usually high, at around 90% for pressure-retarded osmosis.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Electrical energy produced by the Gibbs free energy of mixing.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1776 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1776">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q615"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">thermal gradient energy</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">OTEC</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">deep ocean thermal energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">marine thermal garident energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">ocean heat energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">ocean thermal energy conversion</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">ocean thermal power</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">ocean thermal power conversion</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">thermal gradient power</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">thermal ocean power</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">An organic Rankine thermodynamic cycle can generate electricity using heat transfer between these two water flows. Thermal gradient power is more suitable in places where surface water gets warmer than colder deeper water, such as in tropical regions. As ocean thermal energy conversion systems use thermal energy to generate electricity, there are transformation losses in the system, and the amount of primary energy supplied will be more than the amount of electricity generated. In 2024 generally accepted estimate of what the efficiency of such systems might be existed, but it was expected to be relatively low (under 5%).</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Energy that can be harnessed from the thermal difference between cold deep seawater and warmer surface water with a difference of at least 20°C.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1777 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1777">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q618"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">solar photovoltaic</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">photo-voltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solar PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solar electricity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solar panel</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solar panels</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Solar cells can be arranged together as solar panels and panels can be arranged as arrays. Solar photovoltaic is either connected to an electricity grid or not. Solar photovoltaic devices may also integrate mechanisms, known as concentrator photovoltaic devices, to focus or concentrate solar irradiation onto solar cells, thus increasing their electricity output. High-concentration photovoltaic devices may also need cooling mechanisms for the solar cells. While electricity production from high-concentration photovoltaic devices may resemble concentrated solar power generation, it is classified as solar photovoltaic energy. But any useful heat recovered from cooling water in such devices should be counted in the appropriate subcategory under solar thermal energy. Due to energy access and grid operation planning, the IRENA categorisation subdivides solar photovoltaic into on-grid solar photovoltaic and off-grid solar photovoltaic.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Electricity generated through the direct conversion of solar irradiation into electricity via the photovoltaic effect in semiconductor devices, such as solar cells.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1778 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1778">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q620"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">solar water heating</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solar domestic hot water</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solar hot water</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solar thermal water heating</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solar water energy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solar water power</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Solar water heaters (SWHs) have collectors that absorb the energy from the sun and transfer it to a heat transfer fluid. The heated fluid transfers heat to the domestic water. They are most effective in areas with a high level of solar irradiance and a significant demand for hot water, and they are widely used in residential, commercial and industrial settings.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Conversion of radiative thermal energy from sunlight into thermal energy, which can be used for water heating.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1779 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1779">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q620"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">solar air heating</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solar space heating</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Solar air heating systems typically consist of a collector panel, which absorbs sunlight and heats the air, and potentially a mechanism for circulating the heated air into a building or process. There are three main types of solar air heating process: natural convection, forced convection and radiative heating. The heated air can be used for domestic space heating, ventilation heating, industrial process heating or agricultural drying.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Use of solar thermal energy to heat air.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1780 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1780">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1777"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">utility-scale solar photovoltaic</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">grid connected PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">grid connected photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">grid tied PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">grid tied photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">grid tied solar</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">grid-connected PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">grid-connected photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">grid-tied PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">grid-tied photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">grid-tied solar</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">mains connected PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">mains connected photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">mains-connected PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">mains-connected photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">on-grid solar PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">on-grid solar photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">utility solar</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">utility solar photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">utility-scale solar</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Photovoltaic systems and technologies (e.g. panels, arrays) connected to large-scale electricity networks, allowing excess generation to be fed into the grid. Such systems enable net‐metering or feed‑in tariffs, which may enhance economic viability and encourage distributed energy resources.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Solar photovoltaic systems and technologies connected to the distribution or transmission grid.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1781 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1781">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1777"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">distributed solar</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">autonomous solar</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">independent solar</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">isolated PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">isolated photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">off-grid PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">off-grid photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">off-grid solar PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">off-grid solar photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">stand alone PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">stand alone photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">stand alone solar</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">standalone PV</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">standalone photovoltaic</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">standalone solar</skos:altLabel>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Solar photovoltaic systems and technologies that are not connected to a distribution or transmission grid.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1782 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1782">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1769"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">bio-jet kerosene</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">SAF</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">aviation biofuel</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">aviation biokerosene</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">bio jet fuel</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">bio jet kerosene</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">bio-jet fuel</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">sustainable aviation fuel</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2722"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Bio-jet kerosene can be manufactured from any type of biomass, including plant cellulose and algae. It is produced by a range of thermal processes (including gasification followed by Fischer-Tropsch synthesis or pyrolysis followed by hydrogenation) or by the conversion of sugar to hydrocarbons using micro-organisms such as yeast. Jet fuel is a high-specification fuel that must meet the standards defined in ASTM D1655 for Jet A1 or in the Ministry of Defence Standard 91-91 in the United Kingdom. Bio-jet kerosene must meet these same standards and, in addition, have an ASTM D7566 certification. The ASTM certification procedure is rigorous and can take years and millions of US dollars to complete. Bio-jet kerosene may be blended with other fuels or used directly. Where it is blended, energy statisticians should register a transfer or a transformation, from bio-jet kerosene to blended kerosene.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Liquid biofuel derived from biomass and blended with or replacing jet kerosene.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1783 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1783">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">harvested biomass</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">energy crops</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">firewood</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">fuel wood</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">wood fuel</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Harvested biomass can be used directly or as a feedstock for various bioenergy applications, including combustion for heat and power generation, compression to make biomass pellets and briquettes, and gasification for liquid and gas biofuel production.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Biomass harvested for energy purposes.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1784 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1784">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">harvested residue</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">agricultural residue</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">agricultural residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">agricultural waste</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">agricultural wastes</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">agriculture residue</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">agriculture residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">agriculture waste</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biomass residue</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biomass residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">crop residue</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">crop residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">crop waste</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">crop wastes</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">farm residue</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">farm residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">farm waste</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">farm wastes</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">farming residue</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">farming residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">farming waste</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">farming wastes</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">field residue</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">field residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">harvest residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">harvest waste</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">harvesting residue</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">harvesting residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">harvesting waste</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">plant residue</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">plant residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">residual biomass</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">residual crop</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">residues in fields</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1865"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1952"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2538"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2552"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q572"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Harvest residues can be left on site for nutrient cycling purposes or used to produce liquid biofuels, such as cellulosic ethanol, which can be blended with gasoline to fuel vehicles. Harvest residues can also be burned directly to produce heat and electricity in biomass-fired power plants.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Agricultural residues are materials left on cultivated land after the crop has been harvested.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1785 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1785">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">biomass processing residue</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">bagasse</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">bio mass residue</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">bio mass residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biomass processing residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biomass residue</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biomass residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">black liquor</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">husk</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Includes by-products obtained during the processing of different types of biomass, such as roundwood or agricultural crops, that have significant energy potential. Since these residues are already collected during the processing of biomass, they can be a convenient and inexpensive source of biomass for energy production. These residues can be used for direct combustion or gasification, or they can be further processed into advanced biofuels, such as bio-oil or biogas. Many of these residues have non-energy uses (e.g. as feed, mulch or animal bedding or for the production of reconstituted wood panels), so only the share of these residues used for energy should be counted in energy statistics.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Residue from biomass processing activities across different economic or energy sectors that use solid biofuels as raw materials.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1786 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1786">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">animal residue</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">animal processing residue</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">animal processing residues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">animal residues</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Solid biofuels baed on animal residue consist of by-products from the slaughter and processing of animals. The use of animal processing residues for energy production can provide economic and environmental benefits by reducing waste disposal costs and providing a source of renewable energy. However, their use must comply with regulations regarding waste disposal and public health.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Solid biofuel sourced from animal matter.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1787 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1787">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">biomass pellets</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biomass block</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biomass blocks</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biomass fuel pellet</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biomass fuel pellets</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biomass pellet</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">briquette</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">briquettes</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">compressed biomass</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">fuel block</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">fuel blocks</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">fuel pellet</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">fuel pellets</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">pelletised biomass</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">pelletized biomass</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">wood pellet</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">wood pellets</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Biomass pellets and briquettes are solid, processed biofuels made from dried, cut, crushed and compressed biomass. Wood pellets, created from fuelwood or wood residues, are the most common, but other solid biomass materials such as rice husks, straw, nut shells and renewable municipal waste can also be used. Producing these biofuels involves compressing the biomass or adding a binder, which typically makes up less than 3% of the final weight. These biofuels have low moisture content (below 10%) and high combustion efficiency. Pellets are cylindrical with diameters under 25mm and lengths under 100mm. Briquettes are also cylindrical but can vary in size. Biomass pellets and briquettes are primarily used for heat production (either sold or for direct use) and are increasingly used for electricity generation in countries that rely on imported biofuels to meet bioenergy targets. Torrefied biomass pellets and briquettes, known as black pellets or bio-coal, are not included in this category and should be classified as charcoal. When reporting biomass pellet and briquette use, data on the primary energy sources (solid biofuels) used for production should be included in their respective categories, with the amounts used for manufacturing indicated as other transformation in the energy balance. If available, the renewable content of pellets and briquettes made from refuse-derived fuel and solid recovered fuel can also be included, with the primary input amounts appearing in the statistics for renewable municipal waste.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Secondary energy products that can be produced from one or more biofuels after undergoing physical and/or chemical transformations.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1788 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1788">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">biomass briquette</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1789 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1789">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">charcoal</rdfs:label>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Charcoal mainly consists of carbon, with small amounts of ash and other impurities. Charcoal is typically made from wood (fuelwood and wood residues) but can be made from almost any type of solid biomass. Carbonisation usually involves slow pyrolysis, where biomass is heated in a controlled environment without air for several days (for artisanal charcoal). Alternatively, charcoal can be produced more quickly in an industrial kiln or retort that operates at high temperatures. Heat for the charcoal-making process can come from partial combustion of the original biomass or other energy sources. Charcoal is primarily used for direct heat, with cooking and iron and steel production being the most common uses, where it substitutes for coke. It also has non-energy uses, such as in medicine and filters as activated carbon. Charcoal comes in various forms, depending on the type of primary solid biofuel used, including lump charcoal from fuelwood, charcoal briquettes, bamboo charcoal and charcoal made from compressed wood pellets and briquettes. Additionally, torrefied biomass pellets and briquettes are classified as charcoal and can be used as a substitute for coal and for co-firing in thermal power plants. Torrefied biomass is different from charcoal produced through the traditional slow pyrolysis process, since it involves heating the biomass at lower temperatures and for shorter periods of time.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Secondary solid biofuel obtained through the carbonisation of wood or other vegetable matter in an oxygen-free environment.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


