        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilience of power grid</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilience of power grids</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilience of power system</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilience of power systems</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilience of the grid</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilient electricity grid</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilient electricity grids</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilient energy grid</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilient energy grids</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilient grid</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilient grids</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilient national grid</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilient national grids</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilient power grid</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilient power grids</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilient power system</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilient power systems</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The ability of a power system to resist, respond, and recover from a catastrophic event - such as environmental disasters - are key factors commonly used to define the resilience of the power system. Resilience measures reduce damage from outages and hasten restoration and recovery to shorten outage durations.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">The ability of a power system to resist, respond, and recover from a catastrophic event - such as environmental disasters - are key factors commonly used to define the resilience of the power system. Resilience measures reduce damage from outages and hasten restoration and recovery to shorten outage durations.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1790 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1790">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1770"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">biogas from anaerobic digestion</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">AD biogues</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">anaerobic digestion biogas</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">animal residue biogas</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">digester gas</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">landfill biogas</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">methane from digestion</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">vegetable residue biogas</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">wastewater sludge biogas</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q366"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Anaerobic digestion is a biochemical process that occurs in low-oxygen environments, where organic matter (made up of the elements C-H-O-N-S) decomposes in contact with water to produce methane and carbon dioxide (biogas). The full process consists of four complex biochemical reactions: hydrolysis, acidogenesis, acetogenesis, and methanogenesis. On a large scale, biogases from anaerobic digestion are primarily used for electricity production (including combined heat and power production) or for mixing with natural gas in gas distribution networks. On a small scale, they are more often used to cook (within the heat rising energy sector). When these biogases are blended with other gases, energy statisticians should register this as a transfer or as a transformation from the biogas to the blended gas. If any of the gases are used in the production process, this should be shown as own-use of the gases and not as biogas consumption.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Biogas from anaerobic digestion, primarily consisting of methane and carbon dioxide.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1791 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1791">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1770"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">biogas from thermal processes</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">syngas</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">synthesis gas</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Pyrolysis is a thermochemical process where biomass is heated in the absence or near-absence of oxygen to prevent oxidation. This process produces charcoal, pyrolysis gases, and oils. These outputs can be further processed into bioliquids or biogases or used as a heat source for the pyrolysis itself. The yield of solids, liquids, and gases depends on the pyrolysis temperature and residence time—faster pyrolysis favors gas production. Combustion, by contrast, involves burning biomass at high temperatures with excess air (λ &gt; 1), mainly to generate heat. Complete combustion yields CO₂ and water, though incomplete combustion can produce other by-products. Dust is also formed, consisting of coarse fly ash (containing metals and unburned organics) and aerosols—tiny solid and liquid particles. Gasification lies between pyrolysis and combustion. It heats biomass with limited oxygen (λ &lt; 1), producing carbon-rich dust and biogas. Gasification reactors often include combined zones for drying, pyrolysis, combustion, and carbon reduction. The resulting synthesis gas (&quot;syngas&quot;) typically contains CO, CO₂, and H₂, with possible traces of CH₄ and N₂. Its composition depends on the biomass type and process used. Syngas is versatile: it can be used to generate electricity (including combined heat and power), or to produce biomethane, advanced biofuels, and other non-energy chemicals. However, for statistical energy reporting, only the portion of biogas from thermal processes used to generate electricity or biomethane is counted. When biogas is upgraded to biomethane, only the thermal biogas share should be reflected in energy balances, possibly via a transformation flow.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Biogas from thermal processes produced by the pyrolysis or gasification of solid biomass, or as a by-product of incomplete combustion.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1792 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1792">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1770"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">gas biofuel product</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">RNG</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">bio-DME</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">bio-natural gas</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biohydrogen</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biological hydrogen</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biomethane</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biothermal hydrogen</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">renewable natural gas</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Gas biofuel products can be categorise according to chemical composition. Biological feedstock undergoes a chemical or physical transformation process to produce biomethane, biohydrogen, biodimethyl ether and other biogas products.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Secondary energy products mostly stemming from biogas from anaerobic digestion or biogas from thermal processes.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


