        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Riverine flooding occurs when rivers and streams overflow their banks, flooding nearby land. This happens due to the complex interplay of hydrology, climate, and human management. Factors like heavy rain, snowmelt, and river management practices contribute to this type of flood.</cpr:P7>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q983 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q983">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q949"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">cryosphere risk</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">changes in the cryosphere</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">cryosphere change</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">cryosphere hazard</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">cryosphere-related changes</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">cryospheric changes</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">cryospheric hazards</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">cryospheric risk</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">shrinking cryosphere</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">dsxc3m8e</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1518"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q372"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q974"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The cryosphere is the frozen part of the Earth—it includes sea ice, glaciers, snow, and ice sheets. It helps regulate the climate and weather by reflecting sunlight. Rising temperatures are causing melting, leading to sea ice loss, glacier collapse, and glacier landslides and avalanches. This threatens the livelihoods of around 700 million people who rely on the cryosphere for food, water security, and cultural practices.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">The cryosphere is the frozen part of the Earth—it includes sea ice, glaciers, snow, and ice sheets. It helps regulate the climate and weather by reflecting sunlight. Rising temperatures are causing melting, leading to sea ice loss, glacier collapse, and glacier landslides and avalanches. This threatens the livelihoods of around 700 million people who rely on the cryosphere for food, water security, and cultural practices.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q984 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q984">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q443"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">malnutrition</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">caloric deficiency</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">dietary deficiency</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">energy deficiency</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">food deprivation</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">hidden hunger</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">hunger</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">inadequate nutrition</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">malnourishment</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">micro-nutrient deficiencies</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">micronutrient malnutrition</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">nutrient deficiency</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">nutritional deficiencies</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">nutritional deficiency</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">nutritional imbalance</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">nutritional inadequacy</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">obesity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">overnutrition</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">poor nutritional status</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">protein-energy malnutrition</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">starvation</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">undernourishment</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">undernutrition</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Malnutrition arises from insufficient food intake, poor health, and inadequate care, leading to being underweight, stunted, wasted, or deficient in essential vitamins and minerals. Extreme weather events like floods and droughts can worsen food insecurity and malnutrition by disrupting food production, increasing food prices, and reducing household incomes.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Malnutrition occurs when inadequate food intake and poor health lead to underweight, stunted growth, wasting, or vitamin/mineral deficiencies, impacting overall health.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q985 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q985">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q954"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">cyber impact</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">cyber hazard</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q838"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Data security involves protecting data to ensure its availability, confidentiality, and integrity. Impacts to data security include risks from increased system complexity, which can enable cyberattacks, as well as potential data loss from severe incidents like natural disasters</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Data security ensures data availability, confidentiality, and integrity. Impacts include risks from complex systems enabling cyberattacks and data loss from major incidents</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q986 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q986">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q975"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">geohazard</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">geohazards</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">geological disaster</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">geological hazard</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">geological hazards</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">non-climatic natural disaster</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">non-climatic natural risks</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">mjaupkch</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Geohazards are adverse geological phenomenon&apos;s that are capable of causing widespread damage or loss to property, life and the environment, such as earthquakes, volcano eruptions and tsunamis. Climate change can increase the frequency and severity of some geohazards, such as landslides, and soil erosion.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Geohazards are adverse geological phenomenon&apos;s that are capable of causing widespread damage or loss to property, life and the environment, such as earthquakes, volcano eruptions and tsunamis. Climate change can increase the frequency and severity of some geohazards, such as landslides, and soil erosion.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q987 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q987">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q986"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">seismogenic hazards</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">seismic hazard</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">seismogenic hazard</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1143"/>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">A seismic hazard is the probability that an earthquake will occur in a given geographic area, within a given window of time, and with ground motion intensity exceeding a given threshold</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q988 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q988">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q986"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">volcanogenic hazards</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">volcanogenic hazard</skos:altLabel>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">It refers to any potentially dangerous volcanic process that puts human lives, livelihoods or infrastructure at risk of harm.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q989 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q989">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q954"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">industrial failure</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">industrial accident</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Industrial accidents, such as oil spills from leaks, blowouts, or shipping accidents, pose severe climate risks to ecosystems. These incidents, often exacerbated by extreme weather, threaten biodiversity and disrupt ecosystem health. As climate-related extreme events rise, so does the risk of industrial accidents, including oil spills and explosions.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Industrial accidents like oil spills, often intensified by extreme weather, threaten ecosystems and biodiversity, with risks rising as climate impacts grow.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q99 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q99">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q98"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">disaster risk assessment tools</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">assess disaster risk</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">assessing disaster risk</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">disaster risk assessment</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">disaster risk assessments</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">disaster risk map</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">disaster risk mapping</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">disaster risk maps</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">mapping disaster risk</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">A qualitative or quantitative approach to determine the nature and extent of disaster risk by analysing potential hazards and evaluating existing conditions of exposure and vulnerability that together could harm people, property, services, livelihoods and the environment on which they depend.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">A qualitative or quantitative approach to determine the nature and extent of disaster risk by analysing potential hazards and evaluating existing conditions of exposure and vulnerability that together could harm people, property, services, livelihoods and the environment on which they depend.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q990 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q990">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q954"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">transportation accident</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">transport accident</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Extreme weather events can increase the risks of accidents, disruptions in travel, and lead to road closures or detours, causing delays and logistical challenges.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Extreme weather events can increase the risks of accidents, disruptions in travel, and lead to road closures or detours, causing delays and logistical challenges.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q991 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q991">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q954"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">CBRNE</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">biological agents</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">chemical warfare  agents</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">explosive agents</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">nuclear agents</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">radiation agents</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">radioactive material</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The acronym CBRNE stands for Chemical, Biological, Radiological, Nuclear, and Explosive. It encompasses a range of hazardous materials and situations, often used in the context of emergency response and public safety to describe threats or incidents involving these dangerous agents.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">The acronym CBRNE stands for Chemical, Biological, Radiological, Nuclear, and Explosive. It encompasses a range of hazardous materials and situations, often used in the context of emergency response and public safety to describe threats or incidents involving these dangerous agents.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q992 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q992">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q956"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">armed conflict</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">NIAC</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">civil disorder</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">civil disturbance</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">civil unrest</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">conflict risk</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">environmental degradation from conflict</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">environmental degradation from war</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">explosive remnants of war</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">international armed conflict</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">non-international armed conflict</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">risk of conflict</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">social unrest</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">stampede</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">violent conflict</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">violent disorder</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Conflict risk refers to the increased likelihood of social or political unrest arising from various stressors, including social, economic, and environmental factors. While climate change doesn&apos;t directly cause conflict, it can worsen conditions like resource scarcity, food insecurity, and displacement, increasing tensions and instability, especially in regions with poor governance.</cpr:P7>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">conflict of interest</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">conflict of interests</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">conflicts of interest</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">conflicts of interests</cpr:P9>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Scientists generally agree that climate change does not directly cause armed conflict but can indirectly raise conflict risks by worsening social, economic, and environmental stresses.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q994 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q994">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1017"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">food insecurity</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">acute-food insecurity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">drought-related acute food insecurity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">drought-related food insecurity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">famine</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">flood-related acute food insecurity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">flood-related food insecurity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">food availability</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">food crisis</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">food insecure</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">food resource scarcity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">food scarcity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">food security</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">food shortage</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">food shortages</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">malnutrition</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">nutrition insecurity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">risk of hunger</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">scarcity of food</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">shortage of food</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">undernutrition</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1167"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1599"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1826"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Food insecurity refers to the lack of reliable access to sufficient, safe, and nutritious food. Climate change exacerbates this issue by directly impacting food production, increasing food prices, and reducing household incomes. In addition, climate change frequently acts to compound drivers of food insecurity, such as poverty and inequity.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Food insecurity is the lack of access to enough safe and nutritious food. Climate change worsens it by disrupting food production and increasing prices, impacting vulnerable populations.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q995 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q995">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q975"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">extraterrestrial hazard</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">D region absorption</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Solar energetic particle event</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">UV radiation</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">air-blast</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">airburst</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">bolide</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">electromagnetic pulse</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">extra-terrestrial hazards</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">fireball</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">geomagnetic storm</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">ionospheric storm</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">magnetic storm</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">meteor collision</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">meteor impact</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">meteorite impact</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">near-earth object</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">radio blackout</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">radio disturbance</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solar proton event</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solar radiation storm</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">solar storm</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">space accident</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">space hazard</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">space weather</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">superbolide</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Extraterrestrial  hazards are caused by asteroids, meteoroids, and comets as they pass near to the Earth, enter the Earth’s atmosphere, and/or strike the Earth, and by changes in interplanetary conditions that effect the Earth’s magnetosphere, ionosphere, and thermosphere.</cpr:P7>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">We have not applied extra-terrestrial hazard to our dataset, as it is not relevant to our climate-centred dataset (e.g. meteor impacts).</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Extraterrestrial hazards are caused by asteroids, meteoroids, and comets as they pass near to the Earth, enter the Earth’s atmosphere, and/or strike the Earth, and by changes in interplanetary conditions that effect the Earth’s magnetosphere, ionosphere, and thermosphere.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q996 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q996">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q368"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">ocean deoxygenation</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">aquatic oxygen loss</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">coastal hypoxia event</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">coastal hypoxia events</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">decreased marine oxygen levels</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">hypoxia in the ocean</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">marine deoxygenation</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">marine hypoxia</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">marine oxygen loss</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">ocean oxygen loss</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">oceanic hypoxia</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">open-ocean deoxygenation</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">oxygen depletion in seawater</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">sea oxygen decline</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Ocean deoxygenation is the loss of oxygen in ocean waters due to warming, reducing oxygen saturation, and increasing consumption, stratification, and ventilation changes. Human activities, such as nutrient runoff and pollution, have worsened coastal and open-ocean hypoxia, harming marine organisms and shrinking marine habitats.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Deoxygenation is the reduction of oxygen levels in seawater, primarily due to ocean warming, which decreases dissolved oxygen. It is exacerbated by increased oxygen consumption, stratification, and reduced water mixing.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q997 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q997">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1017"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">water insecurity</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">deficit of water</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">lack of water</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">lack of water availability</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">limited water availability</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">scarcity of water</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">shortage of water</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">shortages of water</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">vulnerable water resources</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">water crisis</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">water deficit</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">water deficits</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">water insecure</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">water resource scarcity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">water scarcity</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">water security</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">water shortage</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">water shortages</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1167"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q441"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q818"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q905"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Water scarcity can be defined as a lack of sufficient water, or not having access to safe water supplies, which is worsened by climate change by disrupting precipitation patterns and the water cycle. Currently, two billion people lack safe drinking water, and about half the world’s population faces severe water scarcity for at least part of the year, as climate impacts intensify.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Water scarcity can be defined as a lack of sufficient water, or not having access to safe water supplies.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q999 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q999">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q368"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q974"/>
